crypto/x509: parse keys on unsupported named curves as unknown

ParseCertificate fails with "x509: unsupported elliptic curve" when a
certificate's id-ecPublicKey key names a curve that crypto/x509 doesn't
implement, such as brainpoolP256r1 (RFC 5639), which some national PKIs
require. The whole certificate is lost, and ParseCertificates fails a
bundle that contains one such certificate. ParseCertificateRequest
fails the same way.

Report such keys like keys with an unknown public key algorithm:
PublicKeyAlgorithm is UnknownPublicKeyAlgorithm and PublicKey is nil, and
the rest of the certificate or request is populated, including
RawSubjectPublicKeyInfo, so callers can handle the key themselves. This
is what ParseCertificate already does for unknown algorithm OIDs, and
for ML-DSA keys under the FIPS 140-3 module v1.0.0.

Malformed ECDSA parameters, implicitCurve (NULL) and specifiedCurve
(explicit parameters, which RFC 5480 forbids) keep failing.
ParsePKIXPublicKey keeps failing too: it has nothing else to return.

Verify results don't change. A chain that Verify returns never contains
a certificate with such a key:

  - Verify rejects a leaf with such a key, on every platform, as before
    when it couldn't be parsed;
  - chain building already skips issuers with a nil key;
  - chains returned by the platform verifiers (Windows, macOS, iOS) that
    contain such a certificate are dropped. Their signatures can't be
    checked by this package, which matters for the extra checks done
    after the Windows verifier.

The new GODEBUG setting x509unknowncurve=0 restores the parse error.

ivory: P1, profiles core and kt. Base: go1.27.2. Upstream: proposal A.
The overlay's table entry is {Name: "x509unknowncurve", Package:
"crypto/x509"}, on whatever the module's Go version; the upstream CL
uses Changed: <minor>, Old: "0" and documents the setting in
doc/godebug.md (not part of the Go distribution that the overlay
patches). runtime/metrics/doc.go is regenerated with
"go generate runtime/metrics".

diff --git a/src/crypto/x509/parser.go b/src/crypto/x509/parser.go
--- a/src/crypto/x509/parser.go
+++ b/src/crypto/x509/parser.go
@@ -306,6 +306,28 @@ func parseExtension(der cryptobyte.String) (pkix.Extension, error) {
 	return ext, nil
 }
 
+// errUnsupportedCurve is returned by parsePublicKey for an ECDSA public key
+// with well-formed parameters that name a curve this package doesn't
+// implement.
+var errUnsupportedCurve = errors.New("x509: unsupported elliptic curve")
+
+var x509unknowncurve = godebug.New("x509unknowncurve")
+
+// isUnsupportedCurve reports whether err, returned by parsePublicKey, is
+// errUnsupportedCurve and the key should be reported as a key with an
+// unknown algorithm. With x509unknowncurve=0, such keys fail to parse, as
+// they did before.
+func isUnsupportedCurve(err error) bool {
+	if err != errUnsupportedCurve {
+		return false
+	}
+	if x509unknowncurve.Value() == "0" {
+		x509unknowncurve.IncNonDefault()
+		return false
+	}
+	return true
+}
+
 func parsePublicKey(keyData *publicKeyInfo) (any, error) {
 	oid := keyData.Algorithm.Algorithm
 	params := keyData.Algorithm.Parameters
@@ -350,7 +372,7 @@ func parsePublicKey(keyData *publicKeyInfo) (any, error) {
 		}
 		namedCurve := namedCurveFromOID(*namedCurveOID)
 		if namedCurve == nil {
-			return nil, errors.New("x509: unsupported elliptic curve")
+			return nil, errUnsupportedCurve
 		}
 		return ecdsa.ParseUncompressedPublicKey(namedCurve, data)
 	case oid.Equal(oidPublicKeyEd25519):
@@ -1094,6 +1116,15 @@ func parseCertificate(der []byte) (*Certificate, error) {
 			Algorithm: pkAI,
 			PublicKey: spk,
 		})
+		if isUnsupportedCurve(err) {
+			// Report keys on named curves that this package doesn't
+			// implement like keys with an unknown algorithm, so that
+			// callers can inspect the rest of the certificate. Verify
+			// rejects such certificates.
+			cert.PublicKeyAlgorithm = UnknownPublicKeyAlgorithm
+			cert.PublicKey, err = nil, nil
+			cert.unsupportedCurve = true
+		}
 		if err != nil {
 			return nil, err
 		}
@@ -1152,6 +1183,13 @@ func parseCertificate(der []byte) (*Certificate, error) {
 
 // ParseCertificate parses a single certificate from the given ASN.1 DER data.
 //
+// If the certificate's public key is an ECDSA key on a named curve that this
+// package doesn't implement, its PublicKeyAlgorithm is
+// UnknownPublicKeyAlgorithm and its PublicKey is nil, as for a public key
+// algorithm this package doesn't implement. [Certificate.Verify] rejects such
+// a certificate. This behavior can be disabled, so that parsing fails, by
+// including "x509unknowncurve=0" in the GODEBUG environment variable.
+//
 // Before Go 1.23, ParseCertificate accepted certificates with negative serial
 // numbers. This behavior can be restored by including "x509negativeserial=1" in
 // the GODEBUG environment variable.
